WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.420
In Hamburger's raw counts, a chick embryo peaks near 20,000 leg motor neurons.

2
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.880
By about day 10, roughly 12,000 are left.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.100
Nothing is wrong with this embryo.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.900
It has both legs, and it will hatch and walk.

5
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.580
So where did the neurons go?

6
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:22.673
Suppose you count only at the end, and the number is low.

7
00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:25.213
Maybe the cord divided too few times.

8
00:00:25.793 --> 00:00:29.013
Maybe it made enough cells, but sent too few to the place you counted.

9
00:00:29.673 --> 00:00:32.113
Maybe too few became the cell type you looked for.

10
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.713
Or maybe the cells were made, and then lost.

11
00:00:35.453 --> 00:00:37.853
A final count looks the same in every case.

12
00:00:38.867 --> 00:00:41.307
To tell these apart, count at many times.

13
00:00:41.787 --> 00:00:44.247
Remove a wing bud from one side of chick embryos.

14
00:00:44.947 --> 00:00:47.267
Then take embryos at closely spaced stages.

15
00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:50.787
In each one, compare the operated side with the intact side.

16
00:00:51.407 --> 00:00:54.947
Count the healthy motor neurons, and count the dying cells, which have dark,

17
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:59.767
condensed nuclei. If the neurons were never made, the operated side is low from the

18
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:01.987
first count, with no burst of dying cells.

19
00:01:02.547 --> 00:01:05.627
If they were made and then lost, the two sides start equal.

20
00:01:06.447 --> 00:01:09.867
Then dying cells appear, and the operated side falls behind.

21
00:01:10.917 --> 00:01:16.657
In 1975, Hamburger counted the death itself, in normal embryos with all their limbs.

22
00:01:17.437 --> 00:01:22.297
The motor neurons that serve the leg peaked at just over 20,000, between days 5.5

23
00:01:22.297 --> 00:01:23.637
and a half and six and a half.

24
00:01:24.157 --> 00:01:27.097
They settled near 12,000 after day 9.5.

25
00:01:27.497 --> 00:01:31.037
That is a loss of about 40%, percent in his uncorrected counts.

26
00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:34.737
Most of it came between days 6.5 and a half and nine and a half.

27
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.840
Hollyday and Hamburger tested the logic from the other direction.

28
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.960
They grafted an extra leg onto chick embryos.

29
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.580
At day 6, before the normal loss, the grafted side had no more motor neurons than

30
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:53.050
the control. Later, in embryos whose grafts moved vigorously, it had more.

31
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.260
About 11% to 28% percent more.

32
00:01:56.233 --> 00:01:58.253
More target did not mean more births.

33
00:01:58.833 --> 00:01:59.933
It meant fewer deaths.

34
00:02:00.693 --> 00:02:04.653
These results suggested that the target supplies something limited that the neurons

35
00:02:04.653 --> 00:02:08.353
need to survive. So a final count hides its own history.

36
00:02:09.073 --> 00:02:13.373
Count across time, and you can tell neurons never made from neurons made and lost.
